Z||000000||Kwez||06-14-2001||09:58 PM||||ok, quick Kweztion :<P>this is advertised as a 87GT, 5speed,<BR>are those wheels 'normal' for a 87GT ??<BR>I'm thinking of checking the car out & was wondering about that ..<P>   <IMG SRC="http://www.enter-net.com/~pquesnel/car/87gt_5vit.jpg">   <P>nb: it's stick shift; about $1500CAD cheaper than the my 86Gt, so I could, maybe, still be a Fiero owner .. instead of just selling (temporary $$ probs) mine I could live w. the trade ;-)<P>oh well, just can't afford an 88 .. much less a 5speed 88 ! ... so I might just get some cheaper 5spd Gt & when i'm friends w. some $$$ again build it up ...<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Kwez (edited 06-14-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||cadaver||06-14-2001||10:23 PM||||Those are not factory for the 87GT. They had the lace style rims. But maybe it had custom rims and they got swapped before it was sold with what ever was around.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Oreif||06-14-2001||10:28 PM||||No those are not the original wheels. The Fastback GT's all came with the 15" lace wheels. Those are the 14" hi-tech wheels. Someone had to put those on. They probably sold the originals and had aftermarket wheels. But didn't want to trade the car in with the aftermarkets so they stuck those on it. The only other thing is it could be an 87 SE (which came with those wheels) but someone could have added the fastback clip on it and painted the aero effects silver. I would check the VIN # prior to buying it.<P>------------------<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">     <BR><B><I>Got Emblems?</I></B><p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 06-14-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Old Lar||06-14-2001||10:33 PM||||As far as I know all fast back styled GT's had the lace wheels that were 7x15.  The car in the photo may have switched wheels, or someone switched body panels onto a coupe.  It looks like the headlight motors are shot (lights up)and there is a lot of visual paint damage to the lower part of the nose.  Get the vin #  the 5th digit in the vin should be a "G" for the GT cars of 85-88.  Fastback styled cars came out in 86.  By the color the car looks like a chewed up 87 GT with the wrong wheels.  Be wary.||||reg||5
Z||000004||Kwez||06-14-2001||11:37 PM||||hmmm: 14", AAAHHH !! ;-)<P>ok, <B>IF</B> I take the time to move my a$$ to go check it, I'll check the VIN for sure.<BR>(hehe, I saved the 'how to Id an 88' post, so I can check the VIN thing .. learning, slowly ... ;-)<P>That's why I asked : too many funny guys that put 86-87Gt panels on a 84 or SEs etc ...<BR>(heck, too many small variants through the model years for me to know what's what / real)<P>I guess I don't care too much about the paint at this point ... the wheels, well I want 16" eventually ...<P><BR>Thanks alot everybody<BR>"I hope I can keep one" Kwez||||reg||1
Z||000005||Monkeyman||06-15-2001||03:44 AM||||Nothing wrong with converting a notchback into a fastback as long as you know what you're actually getting.  Just some points to remember:  the fastback GTs all had the aux gauges, and all (except the early '86s) had the 5 spd Getrag trannies.  Good thing about the Getrags are that they work well.  Bad thing is that, once they don't work well, they're mighty expensive to fix.  $1500 CDN seems a little low for a fastback unless it's in pretty poor condition (what's the conversion??  about a grand US?).  Check the VIN (Carfax works well), mileage, get under the car and visually check suspension, etc. and make sure you do a good test drive.<P>MM||||reg||1
